# 24. Common Content Distribution Mistakes

## Mistake 1: Treating all platforms identically

Different platforms can have different technical and content requirements.

## Mistake 2: Using one content record for every publication state

Each destination should have its own publication status.

## Mistake 3: No content history

Without history, duplicate publication becomes harder to prevent.

## Mistake 4: Mixing content processing with browser execution

Content transformation and publishing have different responsibilities and failure modes.

## Mistake 5: No retry strategy

Temporary failures should be distinguishable from permanent failures.

## Mistake 6: No monitoring

At scale, manually checking every account and publication is inefficient.

## Mistake 7: Measuring output instead of outcomes

The number of published posts is not the same as the effectiveness of a campaign.

---

# 25. Security Considerations

Content distribution systems may handle sensitive information, including account credentials, authentication sessions, API credentials, and private content.

Do not commit sensitive credentials to a public repository.

Separate:

```text id="2j7r8k"
Source Code
     │
     ├── Configuration
     ├── Secrets
     └── Runtime State
```

Use appropriate secret-management mechanisms for production environments.

Content that is not intended for public distribution should also be stored separately from public repository assets.

